<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Maukharis</h1><p class="st-meta">550 CE – 606 CE (approx.) · capital Kannauj</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The dynasty that made Kannauj an imperial city in the 6th century, contesting the Gupta legacy before passing its throne to Harsha.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Maukhari chiefs are known from inscriptions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> from earlier centuries. The line that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a> began as feudatories of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> in the early 6th century. As Gupta power faded, Ishanavarman took the imperial title maharajadhiraja around 554.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ishanavarman claimed victories over the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Andhras</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauda-region">Gaudas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, and the Maukharis fought a long rivalry with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-guptas">Later Guptas</a> for control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a>. They ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-yamuna-doab">Doab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>, which began its rise as the leading city of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. They issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins modelled on Gupta issues and supported Vedic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaiva</a> traditions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grahavarman">Grahavarman</a>, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajyashri">Rajyashri</a>, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a>, but was killed around 606 by the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>. Harsha of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pushyabhuti-dynasty">Pushyabhuti dynasty</a> then took over the Maukhari kingdom and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/maukharis.jpg" alt="Maukharis" width="320" height="155"><figcaption>Image: CNG coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maukharis_of_Kanauj._Isanavarman._Circa_AD_535-553.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>550 CE – 606 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kannauj</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ishanavarman, Sharvavarman, Grahavarman</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Maukhari dynasty, Maukharis of Kannauj</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=578&amp;focus=maukharis" aria-label="Maukharis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6783 1186 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Maukharis lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7103 1442L7362 1550M7103 1442L7103 1442"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7362 1550h0M7103 1442h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7103 1442h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7103 1442h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kannauj. <a href="/atlas?year=578&amp;focus=maukharis">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
